{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"virtual-functions","title":"Virtual Functions & vtables","group":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","order":38,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>virtual</code> function is dispatched on the object's <em>dynamic</em> type at run time — the mechanism behind runtime polymorphism.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch-horizontal\"></i>Static vs dynamic binding</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Shape {\n    virtual double area() const { return 0; }\n    std::string kind() const { return \"shape\"; }        // non-virtual\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;                         // virtual destructor!\n};\nstruct Rect : Shape {\n    double w, h;\n    Rect(double a, double b) : w(a), h(b) {}\n    double area() const override { return w * h; }     // `override`: compiler checks it overrides\n    std::string kind() const { return \"rect\"; }         // hides, doesn't override\n};\nstruct Circle : Shape {\n    double r;\n    explicit Circle(double x) : r(x) {}\n    double area() const override { return 3.14 * r * r; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Shape&gt;&gt; shapes;\n    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;Rect&gt;(2, 3));\n    shapes.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;Circle&gt;(1));\n    for (const auto&amp; s : shapes) std::cout &lt;&lt; s-&gt;kind() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; s-&gt;area() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    Rect r(1, 1);\n    Shape&amp; ref = r;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; ref.area() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; ref.kind() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; r.kind() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>shape 6\nshape 3.14\n1 shape rect</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Call through…</th><th>Non-virtual function</th><th>Virtual function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Object <code>r.f()</code></td><td>Static type</td><td>Static type (= dynamic)</td></tr><tr><td>Pointer/reference to base <code>p-&gt;f()</code></td><td><strong>Static</strong> type (<code>Shape::f</code>)</td><td><strong>Dynamic</strong> type (<code>Rect::f</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>How it works: vptr &amp; vtable</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">vtable per class</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Each class with virtual functions gets one static table of function pointers, one slot per virtual function.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">vptr per object</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Every object carries a hidden pointer (usually the first 8 bytes) to its class's vtable. Constructors set it.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Call</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>p-&gt;area()</code> compiles to: load <code>p-&gt;vptr</code>, load slot <em>k</em>, call it — one or two extra memory loads.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Plain   { int x; void f() {} };\nstruct Virtual { int x; virtual void f() {} virtual ~Virtual() = default; };\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sizeof(Plain) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(Virtual) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // 4 vs 16: vptr (8) + int + padding\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4 16</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i><code>override</code> and <code>final</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>struct Base {\n    virtual void save(int version) const {}\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n};\nstruct Derived : Base {\n    void save(long version) const override {}   // error: doesn't override (parameter type differs)\n};\nint main() {}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Base { virtual void run() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"base\\n\"; } virtual ~Base() = default; };\nstruct Leaf final : Base {                       // no class may derive from Leaf\n    void run() const override { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"leaf\\n\"; }\n};\nstruct Mid : Base { void run() const final { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"mid\\n\"; } };   // run can't be overridden further\nint main() {\n    const Base&amp; b = Leaf{};\n    b.run();\n    Mid{}.run();\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>leaf\nmid</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Virtual destructors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — missing virtual destructor</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Base {\n    virtual void speak() const {}\n    ~Base() { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"~Base\\n\"; }                        // NOT virtual\n};\nstruct Derived : Base {\n    int* data = new int[100];\n    ~Derived() { delete[] data; std::cout &lt;&lt; \"~Derived\\n\"; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Base* p = new Derived;\n    delete p;          // undefined behaviour: only ~Base would run → ~Derived skipped, leak\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Compiler error (-Wall)</figcaption><pre><code>error: delete called on non-final 'Base' that has virtual functions but non-virtual destructor [-Werror,-Wdelete-non-abstract-non-virtual-dtor]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>If a class has any virtual function (or is meant to be deleted through a base pointer), give it a <code>virtual</code> destructor. Otherwise <code>delete basePtr;</code> skips the derived destructor.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-slice\"></i>Object slicing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Animal { virtual const char* sound() const { return \"...\"; } virtual ~Animal() = default; };\nstruct Cat : Animal { const char* sound() const override { return \"meow\"; } };\nvoid byValue(Animal a)            { std::cout &lt;&lt; a.sound() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }   // copies only the Animal part\nvoid byRef(const Animal&amp; a)       { std::cout &lt;&lt; a.sound() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\nint main() {\n    Cat c;\n    byValue(c);\n    byRef(c);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>...\nmeow</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Passing or storing a derived object <em>by value</em> as its base type <strong>slices</strong> off the derived part — virtual calls then use the base version. Pass polymorphic objects by reference or (smart) pointer; store them as <code>std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;Base&gt;&gt;</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Calling virtuals in constructors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Base {\n    Base() { hello(); }                          // dispatches to Base::hello, not Derived's!\n    virtual void hello() const { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"Base::hello\\n\"; }\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n};\nstruct Derived : Base { void hello() const override { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"Derived::hello\\n\"; } };\nint main() { Derived d; d.hello(); }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Base::hello\nDerived::hello</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How are virtual functions implemented?</summary><div><p>Typically with a per-class <em>vtable</em> (array of function pointers) and a hidden <em>vptr</em> in each object pointing to its class's table. A virtual call loads the vptr, indexes the table and calls indirectly. Cost: one pointer per object, an indirect call that can't normally be inlined.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is calling a virtual function from a constructor dangerous?</summary><div><p>While the base constructor runs, the derived part doesn't exist yet and the vptr points to the base vtable, so the call goes to the base version (or is undefined for a pure virtual). The same applies in destructors.</p></div></details></section>"}